From 693c60dbed35e29c0d3ab7d0d79605ca64f39e50 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 21:02:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 57/76] rc3-instr 1/8: capture-safe guard telemetry
 infrastructure

New vllm/v1/worker/guard_telemetry.py, plus three lines elsewhere.

Nine sites, three int64 slots each (n_masked, example row, example value), in
ONE persistent [9, 3] buffer per device. It follows the QSA KV-clip counter
(nvidia/qsa.py:144-152, :205-225), the only instrument in this tree already
proven to survive FULL cudagraph replay:

  * the buffer is allocated eagerly by prealloc(), called from
    GPUModelRunner.__init__, so nothing is allocated on a capturing stream and
    every data_ptr is stable across replays;
  * guarded kernels do nothing but captured atomics, so replay keeps counting;
  * a daemon thread reads on a cadence OFF the engine thread, so no
    device-to-host sync is added anywhere on the step path.

Round 1 allocated a fresh counter per launch and read it back inline with
.tolist(): the D2H aborted capture at startup and, under replay, the Python
host code never ran, so the counter was re-zeroed every step and never read.

Round 3 changes, from ASTRA-GUARDS-R2.md:

R2-2. The reader is no longer started by counter(). It starts from
start_reader(), called in gpu_worker beside enable_gpu_sync_check() -- the
place the tree already declares warmup and first-compile done, after capture.
A .tolist() from a second thread during an active capture is precisely what
cudaStreamCaptureModeGlobal rejects; the clip-counter precedent suggests it
survives, but that reader is operator-gated and this one is on by default, so
starting after capture deletes the question instead of arguing it. prealloc
stays in __init__, so the allocation is still nowhere near a capture.

R2-2 (second half). The reader no longer swallows read failures. A reader that
cannot read is an instrument that is not there; it now logs the exception and
drops that device rather than spinning silently.

R2-3. check_pending's docstring claimed the raise carries the offending step's
scheduler state. That was false and is corrected, with the two real properties
spelled out: the raise lands on a LATER step (the reader is a cadence, so up to
VLLM_E2_GUARD_INTERVAL and hundreds of decode steps after the hit), and it is
rank-LOCAL (counters are per process, so only the firing rank raises and its
PP/TP peers block in recv/allreduce until the NCCL watchdog fires). Operating
rule, now documented in the module: VLLM_E2_GUARD_MODE=warn for long stress
boots, raise for targeted repro only.

R2-7. Device keys are normalised: torch.device("cuda") and
torch.device("cuda:0") are different dict keys, and a miss would allocate
lazily on whatever stream is current -- blocker B1 returning through a typo.

R2-8. check_pending no longer relies on tuple evaluation order and a None
function used as an expression.

Modes unchanged: VLLM_E2_GUARD_MODE=raise|warn|off, default raise, failing
closed with a ValueError on anything else. VLLM_E2_GUARD_INTERVAL default 0.5s.

ROUND 5 (review R3-3). The reader's failure path no longer pops the buffer. It
was the memory-corrupting half of blocker B1 re-entering through the error
handler: counter() hands out transient buf[site] views and under FULL replay
nothing holds one, so popping dropped the only durable reference, the storage
returned to the caching allocator, and the CAPTURED atomics kept writing to the
baked address. It now adds the device to _read_disabled and keeps the tensor
alive -- stop polling it, never stop holding it.

Also adds _ALWAYS_FATAL_SITES: sites whose hit arms the raise regardless of
VLLM_E2_GUARD_MODE. warn is right for a guard whose masked value dies with the
step; it is wrong for one whose masked value has already been written somewhere
durable. Commit 9 registers the rope-position site.

ROUND 6 (review R4-2). The _ALWAYS_FATAL_SITES name over-promises, so the three
limits are now stated where they are read: MODE=off starts no reader and
therefore never arms it (off means the kernels count and nobody looks,
including here); the raise is RANK-LOCAL, so a hit under PP is one RuntimeError
on the firing rank plus peers hanging to the NCCL watchdog, and the evidence is
the WARNING in the firing rank's log rather than the watchdog traceback; and it
is late by up to VLLM_E2_GUARD_INTERVAL, so a boot with a durable-damage site
armed should set 0.05, not the 0.5 default.

The reader's `seen` high-water marks move from a _reader local to module-level
_seen, cleared by reset_for_test. A test that zeroed the counters but left the
marks got NO warning on the next hit, because the new count was below the
remembered maximum -- a test silently measuring nothing.

Release note, now in the module docstring: prealloc() and check_pending() are
wired into the V2 runner only (vllm/v1/worker/gpu/). On a V1 runner the
counters allocate lazily at the first guarded launch and nothing calls
check_pending, so MODE=raise degrades to warn and _ALWAYS_FATAL_SITES stops
nothing. The guards still mask and count on both.

Draft for Astra review. Not merged into any RC.
